WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:07.680
A lot of people find worms… gross. Primitive.&nbsp;
Boring, even. But monstrous? I didn’t think so.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.680 --> 00:00:10.880
I even had one as a pet in&nbsp;
grade school—don’t judge me.&nbsp;

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:12.800
&nbsp;
But alas, humans can’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:19.680
just leave the digesting tubes in peace. Nope, we&nbsp;
have to have: Monster Death Worms. Like Graboids,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.160
Sandworms, and Precambrian Worms.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:27.840
But long before those manifestations,&nbsp;
two particular kinds of killer worms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:34.000
loomed large in global folklore and were recounted&nbsp;
in history books: the Indus Worm and the Mongolian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:35.120
Death Worm.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:40.800
So how can we account for the purported existence&nbsp;
of these creatures? What makes us want to turn the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:47.600
lowly worm… scary? Or are there actually enormous,&nbsp;
terrifying worms out there, roaming the earth?

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:48.800
You’re about to find out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:55.120
&nbsp;
[Monstrum intro.]

00:00:55.120 --> 00:01:00.640
​​By my definition, the “death worm” of myth,&nbsp;
fiction, and folklore refers to any soft- bodied,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:06.080
limbless invertebrate monster or cryptid that&nbsp;
moves by crawling, lives at least partially under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:08.960
sediment, and poses a threat to humans.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:12.720
The most ancient death worm&nbsp;
on record is the Indus Worm.&nbsp;

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:15.760
&nbsp;
In the 5th century BCE book Indica,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:20.960
Greek physician and historian Ctesias wrote&nbsp;
that the “only animal” found in the Indus River&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:26.960
was a creature that resembled the larvae that&nbsp;
live in fig-trees, but on a much larger scale.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:31.920
He described a behemoth white maggot with a&nbsp;
girth “so wide that a ten-year-old child could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:39.120
hardly embrace it” – and a length spanning “seven&nbsp;
cubits long”. Which, by the way, is close to 3.2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:42.960
meters [or 10.5 feet]. So this thing was huge.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:49.040
He stated that this giant worm—called the skōlex&nbsp;
by his Greek contemporaries—lived obscured in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:54.560
mud, coming out only at night to hunt. According&nbsp;
to his account, the creature had only two teeth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.560 --> 00:02:01.360
in its jaws, one above, one below. It used these&nbsp;
teeth to grasp horses, oxen, and camels—dragging&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:06.000
their bodies into the water to drown before&nbsp;
consuming everything but the intestines.&nbsp;

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.120
&nbsp;
Ctesias wrote that although a difficult endeavor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:15.200
these worms could be captured. A young goat or a&nbsp;
lamb would be chained to a large hook and used as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:20.560
bait. He also claimed that if one was killed&nbsp;
and left out to dry it would secrete a highly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:25.760
flammable oil. Other legends say that the&nbsp;
oil was used for a flame-thrower weapon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:30.000
akin to the Greek fire used by the&nbsp;
Byzantine Empire to torch enemy ships.

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:36.160
A couple of hundred years later, another famous&nbsp;
Greek philosopher, Ptolemy also wrote about a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:41.760
“giant worm” of the Indus, largely repeating&nbsp;
Ctesias’s description, even down to the part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:44.160
about it being “seven cubits tall.”
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:48.320
It wasn’t until the 19th century that&nbsp;
scholars increasingly attributed these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:53.280
stories of the Indus worm to exaggerated&nbsp;
encounters with crocodiles or alligators.&nbsp;

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:55.280
&nbsp;
Which could make sense given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.280 --> 00:03:01.120
that estuarine crocodiles are common across India&nbsp;
and southeastern Asia, with a habitat that spreads&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:06.640
into the Solomon Islands and northern Australia.&nbsp;
Their widespread numbers can be accounted for in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.640 --> 00:03:12.560
part because they can live in marine, brackish,&nbsp;
and freshwater habitats, and they can even cross&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:18.720
the open ocean. And yes, they are known to inhabit&nbsp;
rivers. Perhaps this common predator, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:23.600
reaches anywhere between 3 to 5 meters (9.85-16.4&nbsp;
feet) in length when full grown , is the true&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:26.720
template for our Death Worm of the Indus River.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:31.920
But wait. There is another proposed explanation&nbsp;
for the monster, one rooted in the religious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:37.200
history of the Indus River Valley. In&nbsp;
1852, German scholar Christian Lassen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:42.560
suggested that the worm story was of mythological&nbsp;
origins, serving as an allegory for the gift of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.560 --> 00:03:48.320
fire given by the serpent god of the region, which&nbsp;
Ctesias had misinterpreted as a real creature.&nbsp;

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:51.040
&nbsp;
This theory gains support when we consider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.040 --> 00:03:56.560
all the ancient carved and painted figures of&nbsp;
snakes and cobras, including representations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:01.760
of the semi-divine half-cobra, half-human Naga,&nbsp;
that have been discovered in the Indus Valley.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:06.640
These artifacts suggest that serpents&nbsp;
were indeed associated with the religious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.640 --> 00:04:12.160
traditions of the area. The Nāga were said to live&nbsp;
underground, could blast flames, and possessed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.160 --> 00:04:18.080
magical fire. Moreover, in the Hindu and Buddhist&nbsp;
traditions the Nagas, like the Indus Worms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:24.080
are associated with water. And what if the two&nbsp;
top-and-bottom teeth of the worm are really just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:26.640
distortions of a snake’s fangs?
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:30.560
I’m not sure we’ll ever know the&nbsp;
real origin of the Indus Worm tales.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:34.880
Stories of the creature largely faded&nbsp;
into obscurity after the 19th century,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:39.120
but another killer worm quickly&nbsp;
rose up —the Mongolian Death Worm.

00:04:39.840 --> 00:04:44.720
Already a creature of regional lore,&nbsp;
American explorer and naturalist Roy Chapman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:51.600
Andrews revealed the Mongolian Death worm’s,&nbsp;
terrifying form to a western audience in 1926,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:54.680
in his expedition accounts of the Gobi&nbsp;
desert [On the Trail of Ancient Man].&nbsp;

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:55.600
&nbsp;
Andrews wrote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.600 --> 00:05:00.880
that while he was obtaining permissions for an&nbsp;
expedition, the Prime Minister of Mongolia asked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:07.040
him to capture a specimen of the legendary beast.&nbsp;
Andrews claims that Mongolian locals told him the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:11.920
monster is “shaped like a sausage about two feet&nbsp;
long” [put meters on screen] with no head, tail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:17.760
or legs. It is so poisonous that touching it&nbsp;
or even just looking directly at it would mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:19.360
instant death.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:24.880
In 1932, Andrews clarified that the creature&nbsp;
was “probably an entirely mythical animal.”

00:05:25.520 --> 00:05:29.920
As the legend grew, so did the length of the&nbsp;
beast. Current accounts give the creature’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:35.040
length as between .5 and 1.5 meters [2&nbsp;
to 5 feet] and the legendary red worm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.040 --> 00:05:40.320
now possesses&nbsp; a deadly acidic venom and&nbsp;
is even capable of producing electricity.

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:46.480
In a really bizarre entry into death worm&nbsp;
lore, one noted scientist paid homage to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.480 --> 00:05:50.960
the legendary monster with a prank&nbsp;
in an otherwise very serious journal.&nbsp;

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:53.520
&nbsp;
In 1956, Charles Bogert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:59.360
co-authored a monograph about the Helodermatidae&nbsp;
lizard family that referenced a “new species” of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:05.680
lizard-cobra hybrid “shaped like a sausage” with&nbsp;
a “speckled band” pattern and “fangs in both upper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:09.360
and lower jaws.” Touching its skin will result&nbsp;
in instant death. It is named as the Sampoderma&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:15.920
allergorhaihorhai and lives throughout India, or&nbsp;
“possibly Baker Street England.” Yes, in addition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.920 --> 00:06:20.240
to clearly linking the fictional lizard with&nbsp;
the Mongolian Death Worm, there’s a Sherlock&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:25.760
Holmes reference. Why did a renowned scientist&nbsp;
play such a practical joke, and how did it make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:31.280
its way into a legitimate scientific publication?&nbsp;
Well, you’ll have to ask the peer reviewers about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:36.400
that last part, but reportedly Bogert just&nbsp;
wanted to rile up his uptight colleagues.

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:40.240
The 20th-century continued to&nbsp;
yield more fictional monstrous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.240 --> 00:06:43.120
worms in the pages and films of science fiction.&nbsp;

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:49.840
Frank Herbert’s widely praised Dune series quickly&nbsp;
captured the imaginations of millions of readers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:53.520
and massive critical acclaim.&nbsp;
It also gave us Kyle MacLachlan.

00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:00.000
Center to the story are the Sandworms, a species&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:04.720
of hundreds of meters long ‘worm-like’&nbsp;
monsterS with thick, scaled orange skin.&nbsp;

00:07:05.440 --> 00:07:10.480
A biography of the author written by his son&nbsp;
notes that many mythological tales inspired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:14.480
the creation of Dune including&nbsp;
dragon myths. Throughout history,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:18.960
dragons have been known by many names,&nbsp;
including the Old English ‘wyrm,” which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.960 --> 00:07:23.600
is actually where we get the name for the&nbsp;
very undragon-like invertebrate “worm.”&nbsp;

00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:29.360
And while the Sandworm is also associated with&nbsp;
the religion of Arrakis’s locals and can be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.360 --> 00:07:34.960
captured with hooks and mined for its “spice,”&nbsp;
rather than a flammable oil like the Indus Worm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:38.400
similarities between those two death&nbsp;
worms are interesting as well.&nbsp;

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:43.840
Herbert’s books are vague about the innermost&nbsp;
biology of the Sandworm, perhaps intentionally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:48.240
choosing to focus on how it impacts the&nbsp;
environment of Arrakis and people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:52.480
encounter it.&nbsp; Although the creature&nbsp;
most physically resembles earthworms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.480 --> 00:07:57.280
in the real world ‘annelid’ worms require&nbsp;
moisture to survive which is very different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.280 --> 00:08:01.840
from the dry desert of Herbert’s alien planet.&nbsp;
You would think that the desert habitat and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:06.560
physical similarities of Herbert’s sandworm,&nbsp;
including that it is trailed by lightning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.560 --> 00:08:10.240
would indicate that legends of the&nbsp;
Mongolian Death Worm inspired the author&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:16.800
—yet he attributes the sand dunes of Florence,&nbsp;
Oregon as his primary inspiration. Although&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:21.520
Herbert did conduct extensive research on&nbsp;
deserts and the religions in desert environments.

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:26.960
He also drew parallels between the brutal&nbsp;
shifting sands of the desert with the equally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:32.480
merciless sea. The similarities made in the&nbsp;
book between the sand dunes and the ocean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.480 --> 00:08:38.400
call to mind the real marine worms that mirror&nbsp;
many elements of the Sandworm— “Bobbit worms.”&nbsp;

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:43.520
Distributed across the shallow marine and&nbsp;
intertidal environments of the Atlantic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:48.880
Indian, and Pacific Oceans,&nbsp; Bobbit Worms [Eunicid&nbsp;
polychaetes] are largely mysterious—and pure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:54.400
nightmare fuel if you ask me. What we do know&nbsp;
is that they can grow up to 3 cm in diameter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.400 --> 00:08:59.840
and reach a length of 3 meters. They live in&nbsp;
burrows which they use to hide from their prey&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:05.360
before launching upwards to violently seize&nbsp;
unsuspecting marine critters in their jaws&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:07.840
and drawing them back underground. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:15.760
jaws. Worms with jaws. Who are real. Yeah, these&nbsp;
seem like the real-life version of death worms.

00:09:15.760 --> 00:09:19.440
Let’s not forget Tremors, a franchise&nbsp;
I hold near and dear to my heart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:23.360
whose subterranean worm-like&nbsp;
‘Graboids’ prowl the sands of Nevada.&nbsp;

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:29.760
The special effects artists of the film stated&nbsp;
in a 2014 interview that the “best material”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:35.360
for monsters comes from Nature. They cite the&nbsp;
snapping turtle’s jaws as the inspiration for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:40.720
the Graboids’s mandible. Although again...these&nbsp;
seem more like “Bobbit worms” to me.&nbsp;

00:09:40.720 --> 00:09:46.400
Graboids are pure fiction. Although we do live on&nbsp;
a planet with Worm lizards (Amphisbaenia), usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:50.800
limbless burrowing lizards that resemble&nbsp;
earthworms though they possess rudimentary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:56.000
eyes and eardrums, and are capable of&nbsp;
identifying some low-frequency vibrations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.000 --> 00:10:03.840
Which just so happens to remind me of a&nbsp;
famous fictional species of death worms.

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:10.000
To add to your mental file of real “worm-like&nbsp;
things” that could star in horror movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:14.160
we have grey-brown limbless&nbsp;
burrowing lizards, slowworms.

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:17.360
And then there are the giant earthworms.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:21.840
Scientists theorize that Giant terrestrial&nbsp;
earthworms once roamed parts of our world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.840 --> 00:10:27.760
—and some still do. Like the Giant Gippsland worm&nbsp;
of Australia, which can reach up to 3 meters in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.760 --> 00:10:32.080
length. That being said, these creatures&nbsp;
are much skinnier and lack the killing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.080 --> 00:10:36.960
power of your typical death worm monsters.&nbsp;
Australia also happens to have a few species&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:41.840
of burrowing beach worms that can grow quite long.&nbsp;
Because of course they do. Because, Australia.&nbsp;

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:46.560
So we have thousands of years of worm-like&nbsp;
monsters that inspire monster legends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.560 --> 00:10:52.960
science-fiction, and horror stories—but what&nbsp;
makes these worms scary besides their giant size?&nbsp;

00:10:53.520 --> 00:10:58.320
Some people do suffer from Scoleciphobia,&nbsp;
the irrational fear of worms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.320 --> 00:11:02.640
usually associated with a perception that&nbsp;
they indicate poor hygiene or dirtiness.&nbsp;

00:11:03.440 --> 00:11:09.840
And worms do love the dirt. More broadly&nbsp;
worms, grubs, maggots—all limbless, faceless,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.840 --> 00:11:15.680
wriggling things that can cause us harm. Worms can&nbsp;
contaminate our food. They can enter our bodies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:21.520
They help eat dead things—even us. Perhaps&nbsp;
the persistent construction of Death Worms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.520 --> 00:11:27.360
is partially an extension of this uneasiness about&nbsp;
invertebrates, and a fear of what lies below our&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:28.800
feet every day.&nbsp;
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:33.920
Is there a real, venomous monster worm buried&nbsp;
somewhere in the Gobi Desert or the Indus River&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:40.080
waiting for us to discover it? If you ask me,&nbsp;
probably not. But when we actually start exploring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:45.840
that possibility, we are confronted with the&nbsp;
other very real creatures that wiggle, slither,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.840 --> 00:11:48.160
and creep their way across our planet.
&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:51.120
And sometimes, truth is stranger than fiction.

